---
// ── SEO COPY REWRITE APPLIED, 16 September 2026 ───────────────────
//
// This page has been rewritten against
// aged-care-and-retirement-facilities-copy-rewrite.md (workbook row 38,
// primary `aged care cleaning services`). Section numbers in the comments
// below are that brief's.
//
// Implemented: the approved title, the approved meta with ONE substitution
// explained below, a new H1 carrying the mapped phrase with the old one
// demoted into the hero lead, the mapped phrase in the opening paragraph and
// the same one-word substitution there, the approved CTA in all three places,
// a comparison table rebuilt for this audience (brief §19), the renamed FAQ
// headings, the em dash recast in branded FAQ 3 and the full-footprint town
// list.
//
// THE MAPPING, recorded because these decisions get read months later. Row 38
// was matched to this page ON THE USER'S INSTRUCTION, 16 September 2026. The
// automatic match refused it, because the live page adds "and seniors" to the
// workbook's "Aged care and retirement facilities" and a separate RESIDENTIAL
// row 21, "Senior home cleaning", carries its own 460/mo cluster. §3 of this
// page resolves that better than any rewrite could: it is a routing block
// that tells a family member looking for help for a parent at home that they
// are on the wrong page and sends them to the seniors page. So this page is
// unambiguously the commercial one, row 38 is the right target, and there is
// no cannibalisation with row 21 because the page actively prevents it.
//
// ⚠️ THIS ROW HAS NO MAPPED DEMAND AT ALL. The primary reads 0/mo and there
// are NO secondary keywords whatsoever — the thinnest row in the workbook —
// so the secondary keyword score cannot even be computed. Read that as a data
// gap to re-pull, not as evidence the page has no search value: the Northern
// Rivers has a substantial retirement and aged care population and aged care
// cleaning is a real procurement category nationally. No number is invented
// here, because there is no demand connector available to take one from. The
// practical consequence is the same as on strata: on a page with no usable
// demand data the brand and conversion work IS the work, which is why
// nineteen of the brief's sections are marked No change.
//
// 🔴 THE ONE BLOCKING ITEM, AND WHY THE META AND THE HERO ARE NOT VERBATIM.
// The workbook's approved meta reads "...by police-checked staff who work the
// same site each week", and the live hero said "A screened local team". §7 of
// this page tells an operator, in its own words, that "a cleaning company
// that answers 'our team is all police-checked' without being able to produce
// the evidence per person, on request, has just made their problem your
// problem" — and branded FAQ 2 then asks the same question about TLB and
// answers it with an open bracket. BFD §2.4 settles it independently: police
// checks are marked TBC and "none of them appears in any copy until
// confirmed."
//
// That is the sharpest self-contradiction in this engagement, and it is on
// the page where screening matters most, because the reader is an approved
// provider who is personally accountable for who works around residents. So
// the claim is not placed and flagged, it is replaced, in all three places,
// with the brief's own supplied alternative: "employed", which IS confirmed,
// which §7 argues is what makes screening evidenceable at all, and which is
// the process promise §2.4 asks for. One word, and it is the difference
// between a claim TLB can stand behind and one it cannot.
//
// TO RESOLVE: confirm the screening PER PERSON, with a renewal interval and
// evidence that can be produced on request. Then the approved meta goes in as
// written, FAQ 2 gets a real answer, §7 becomes a promise TLB can keep rather
// than a trap it walks into, and the screening row comes back into the
// comparison table below. ⚠️ THE SAME CONFLICT EXISTS ON THE MEDICAL PAGE,
// workbook row 31. Resolve both together.
//
// Not done here, because they are not copy: the URL mismatch (the workbook
// wants /commercial-cleaning/aged-care-cleaning; the live slug contains no
// form of the word cleaning and its "and-seniors" element points at the
// residential audience this page spends §3 sending away — a redirect decision
// for the build team), the canonical domain, FAQ schema, the two sections
// that still end without a CTA button, the header's competing "Get an instant
// quote", and hiding the
// testimonial block until real quotes exist.
//
// Aged care, retirement and seniors — one of the THIRTEEN "by type of
// premises" leaf pages under the /commercial-cleaning/ hub (navigation.ts,
// "By type of premises" group). Built in one pass with its twelve siblings.
//
// NO CONTENT BRIEF EXISTS for any of the thirteen. All copy below is DRAFTED,
// not supplied. See src/pages/commercial-cleaning/office-cleaning.astro's header for the launch
// blockers shared across the whole cluster.
//
// ⚠️ THE NAMING PROBLEM, and it is the reason §3 exists.
// navigation.ts already flags this in its own source: this page is
// COMMERCIAL cleaning of aged care PREMISES — a residential village, a
// retirement complex, an independent living unit block — bought by an
// operator or a village manager. It is NOT the domestic cleaning of an older
// person's own home under a Home Care Package or DVA, which
// content-plans/home-cleaning.md §3 links to as /house-cleaning/senior-home-cleaning/ and
// which currently has no mega-menu entry and no page.
//
// The menu label says "and seniors", so a family member searching for help
// for their mum will land here. Sending them away in the first screen is the
// single most valuable thing this page does, and §3's Callout does it before
// any commercial copy. Once /house-cleaning/senior-home-cleaning/ exists, that Callout's
// href stops being a dead link — until then it is the ONE KNOWN 404 this
// page ships with, deliberately, because the alternative is silently keeping
// a reader on the wrong page.
//
// WHAT MAKES THIS PAGE DIFFERENT from its twelve siblings, structurally:
//   - `Callout tone="note"` in position three, above everything commercial.
//     No sibling interrupts itself that early. Here it has to.
//   - `StoryMosaic theme="dark"` on cleaning around people who live there,
//     which is this premises type's whole difficulty and has no analogue on
//     an office or a warehouse page.
//   - The scope block is grouped by INFECTION RISK, not by frequency (the
//     office page's grouping) or by area (strata's). That is the grouping an
//     aged care operator's own cleaning policy uses.
//
// BLOCKS LAUNCH, specific to this page:
//   - Does TLB do aged care work today? Nothing in the brand foundation
//     document mentions it. This may be ambition rather than a service.
//   - POLICE CHECKS ARE NOT OPTIONAL HERE. The cluster-wide
//     [police-checked] bracket is a launch blocker everywhere; on this page
//     it is a gate. Aged care providers are required to screen people who
//     work with residents, and an operator will ask for evidence before a
//     walkthrough, not after. If TLB cannot evidence screening for every
//     cleaner, this page should not ship at all.
//   - Whether TLB will take single independent living units, or only whole
//     sites. Bracketed in branded FAQ 1, and it changes who this page is for.
//   - Nothing on this page claims accreditation, an Aged Care Quality
//     Standards audit history, or clinical cleaning capability. Do not add
//     one without evidence: those are auditable claims.
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../components/sections/SiteHeader.astro';
import Hero from '../../components/sections/Hero.astro';
import TrustBar from '../../components/sections/TrustBar.astro';
import Backdrop from '../../components/ui/Backdrop.astro';
import TextBlock from '../../components/sections/TextBlock.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/sections/Callout.astro';
import ServiceBlocks from '../../components/sections/ServiceBlocks.astro';
import StoryMosaic from '../../components/sections/StoryMosaic.astro';
import TagCloud from '../../components/sections/TagCloud.astro';
import ComparisonTable from '../../components/sections/ComparisonTable.astro';
import GoogleReviewSlider from '../../components/sections/GoogleReviewSlider.astro';
import { googleBusinessProfileUrl, googleReviews } from '../../data/googleReviews';
import Faq from '../../components/sections/Faq.astro';
import CallToAction from '../../components/sections/CallToAction.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../../components/sections/SiteFooter.astro';
import logoImg from '../../assets/logo/tlb-logo-horizontal.png';
// This page's own frames are in src/assets/commercial_cleaning/premises/;
// the prompts are in that folder's IMAGE-PROMPTS.md. Both were generated
// warm and homely rather than clinical, on purpose: a village corridor that
// photographs like a hospital corridor sells the wrong service.
// REUSED from the hub's folder per IMAGE-GUIDELINES §5. The subject is the
// walkthrough conversation where a scope gets agreed, which is exactly what
// the hub uses this frame to mean, and the label is the hub's verbatim.
// §2 backdrop, behind the Cream scrim — the homepage's, same as the hub.
import footerLogoImg from '../../assets/logo/tlb-logo-square.png';
import {
  primaryNav,
  headerNav,
  headerSecondaryCta,
  footerServiceLinks,
  footerContact,
  footerCopyright,
  quoteCta,
} from '../../data/navigation';
import { comparisonColumns } from '../../data/comparison';
import { footprintLocationGroups, footprintSubheading } from '../../data/locations';
import { trustBarCards } from '../../data/trust';
import { commercialBusiness, relatedPremises, walkthroughCtaLabel } from '../../data/premises';
import { howItWorks, managedForYou, theLineBody } from '../../data/brand-lines';
import { z } from 'astro/zod';
import { getPage } from '../../lib/cms';
import * as section from '../../lib/cms-schemas';
import { sized } from '../../lib/cms-schemas';

// §0 Metadata. The title leads with "aged care and retirement village
// cleaning" rather than the menu's "and seniors", because the menu label is
// what causes the confusion §3 exists to fix and the meta description is the
// first chance to prevent the wrong click.
//
// In `pageMeta`, on "Aged Care Cleaning Services | TLB Cleaning":
// Brief §2. The approved title, verbatim, at 41 characters. It carries
// `aged care cleaning services` contiguously where the live one split it
// with "and Retirement Village".
//
// ⚠️ CHANGE REQUEST for the workbook owner, brief §2: the approved title
// drops "retirement village" entirely and uses only 41 of 60 characters.
// Retirement villages and independent living units are a distinct buyer
// from residential aged care, they are named throughout the page, and there
// are nineteen characters spare. Pasted as approved per Rule 3, and worth a
// decision, especially if the cluster is being re-pulled anyway. The kicker
// below keeps retirement living named above the fold in the meantime.
// In `pageMeta`, on `description`:
// Brief §3, with ONE substitution: "police-checked" becomes "employed", for
// the reason set out at length in the file header. 141 characters. The live
// meta was 197 — the longest in the engagement — and would have been cut off.
//
// 🔴 Restore the approved wording the moment screening is confirmed.
//
// ⚠️ SECOND CHANGE REQUEST, brief §3: the approved meta does not contain the
// mapped phrase `aged care cleaning services` at all — it says "Cleaning for
// aged care and retirement facilities" — so even once implemented the meta
// component cannot score.
//
// What is lost either way: the live meta ended on "residents treated like
// neighbours", which is the truest line about this page and the thing that
// separates TLB from a national contractor. Neither version of the approved
// meta keeps it. Worth returning to if the workbook owner revisits this row.
// In `pageMeta`, on `canonical`:
// ⚠️ Placeholder domain, same as every sibling page. Confirm before launch.

// §9 The primary CTA, defined once and repeated in three places per BFD
// §9.5: the hero button, the button after the six scope blocks and the
// closing block. The brief counts two, because the live render has no scope
// section button; this build already had one, so §14 ("if a button is added
// here, it takes the approved wording") is satisfied rather than pending.
//
// NO CONFLICT on this page. The approved label fits this buyer better than
// the live one did, because an operator or a board is procuring rather than
// booking, and the closing block already promises a written scope, a price
// per visit and a schedule.
//
// ⚠️ Still short on CTAs against the rest of the cluster, which carries four
// or five. The routing block and the two-questions section both end without
// one. A conversion gap rather than a copy error, and a template job. Brief
// §9.

// §1 Hero. `split-single-image` with the image LEFT, mirroring the office
// page's right. Reading order puts the photo first here on purpose: the
// reader's real question is whether this looks like the place their residents
// live, and the corridor answers it faster than the lead does.
//
// ⚠️ GENERATED, NOT PHOTOGRAPHED. Same standing note as every premises page.
//
// In `hero`, on "Aged care and retirement living":
// Brief §7. The kicker does not change: it keeps retirement living named
// above the fold, which the approved title drops.
// In `hero`, on `headingLines`:
// Brief §4. The exact phrase `aged care cleaning services` is worth 15
// points scored present or absent, and the extension is the page's actual
// argument, taken from its own hero paragraph and from §6.
//
// ON DEMOTING THE OLD LINE. "People live here. We clean like it." is the
// best headline on the site: six words, and it states a whole operating
// philosophy. It is NOT being cut. It lands one line lower, opening the
// lead, and it still does the same work — the new H1 above it says the same
// thing in the words a procurement search uses.
// In `hero`, on `lead`:
// Brief §5 and §6, in that order, with the demoted H1 opening the lead. Hero
// has no separate subheading slot, so the two run as one lead paragraph.
//
// 🔴 "AN EMPLOYED LOCAL TEAM", NOT "A SCREENED LOCAL TEAM". The brief
// supplies both versions and says to use this one unless and until the
// screening is confirmed: "a screened local team" is the same unverified
// claim as the meta, sixty words into the page, and BFD §2.4 bars it from
// copy until confirmed. "Employed" is confirmed, it is what §7 says makes
// screening evidenceable in the first place, and it is the process promise
// §2.4 asks for. See the file header.

// §2 Definition. Answer-target block. Paragraph two draws the line the whole
// page depends on, in the reader's own words, before §3 acts on it.

// §3 The redirect. THE MOST IMPORTANT BLOCK ON THIS PAGE, and the reason it
// sits above every piece of commercial copy.
//
// `note`, not `warning` — it corrects a belief the reader arrived with
// (that this page is about their parent), which is exactly the line
// Callout.astro's docs draw between the two tones.
//
// ⚠️ KNOWN 404. /house-cleaning/senior-home-cleaning/ is referenced by
// content-plans/home-cleaning.md §3 and does not exist yet. Linking a dead
// page is the lesser evil here: a reader who needs the other service is
// better served by a named destination they can search for than by being
// kept on a page written for an operator. When that page ships, this href
// starts working with no change needed. If the decision is that it will
// never ship, change this to /house-cleaning/ rather than deleting the
// block.

// §4 Scope, grouped BY INFECTION RISK rather than by frequency or by area.
// That is the grouping an aged care operator's own cleaning policy uses, and
// it is the one that makes a scope auditable: high-touch, wet areas,
// everything else. No sibling page groups this way.
//
// ⚠️ EVERY ITEM IS DRAFTED against standard practice, not supplied by TLB.
// Confirm each one, and be conservative: this is the page where an
// overstated scope has the worst consequences.
//
// ⚠️ Nothing below claims clinical or infection-control cleaning
// certification, and the last item says out loud what we do not do. Do not
// soften that item to win work.

// §5 Cleaning around people who live there. `StoryMosaic theme="dark"` —
// this premises type's whole difficulty, and it has no analogue on the office
// or warehouse pages. Array order IS the layout (see SECTIONS.md).

// §6 "Worth knowing". On this page the lever is not price, it is screening
// and turnover — the two things an operator is actually accountable for.
//
// ⚠️ VERIFICATION PASS REQUIRED, and be careful here:
//   - Aged care providers in Australia are required to screen people who
//     work with residents, and police certificate or worker screening
//     requirements apply to contractors as well as employees. Correct in
//     substance. NO SECTION, ACT OR SCHEME NAME IS QUOTED on purpose — the
//     screening framework has changed names and thresholds more than once
//     and an undated specific goes stale silently.
//   - "Ask for evidence, not assurance" is the useful, safe version of the
//     point and it is what the paragraph lands on.
//   - Nothing here states TLB's own screening status. That is the
//     [police-checked] bracket, and on this page it is a gate rather than a
//     to-do. See the file header.

// §7 Related pages, with this page filtered out of its own band.
const relatedTags = relatedPremises('/commercial-cleaning/aged-care-cleaning/');

// §9 Comparison table — REBUILT FOR THIS AUDIENCE, brief §19.
//
// The lead was always right: it is the best framing line of any comparison
// table on the site and it converts the table from marketing into
// procurement. The ROWS underneath it did not deliver on it. They were the
// shared residential set, unchanged, on a page about aged care facilities:
// the subject label asked "who cleans your home" and one row was about
// holiday lets. An operator or a board reading an intro about compliance and
// then finding a row about holiday turnovers concludes the page was assembled
// rather than written, and this is the page where that impression is most
// expensive.
//
// Every row below is drawn from something this page already says in prose, so
// no new claim is introduced: the attendance and employment rows from §6 and
// §7, the personal care and clinical waste rows from the sixth scope block and
// non-branded FAQ 6, the scope row from the scope section's own intro, and the
// periodic row from the fifth scope block. Each cell stays factual and neutral
// per BFD §9.4, describing a delivery model rather than accusing anyone.
//
// The COLUMNS stay the shared, approved ones from data/comparison.ts so this
// table cannot drift from the site's others. The brief's own column names ("a
// national contractor", "a local sole operator") describe the same two models
// and its cell values read correctly under either heading.
//
// 🔴 ONE ROW IS HELD BACK, and this is the blocking item in the file header.
// The brief's table includes a "Screening evidence" row reading "Held per
// person, and produced on request". That is exactly the claim BFD §2.4 bars
// until confirmed, and the brief is explicit: if it is not confirmed, DELETE
// the row rather than softening it, because a vague version of this row on
// this page is worse than none. It is deleted. Add it back — verbatim — the
// moment screening is confirmed, and it becomes the strongest row in the
// table.

// §10 Branded FAQs, in the CUSTOMER'S voice — here that is an operator, a
// village manager or a board member.
//
// In `brandedFaqItems`, on "Do you take single units, or only whole sites?" (answer):
// ⚠️ Real bracket, and it decides who this page is for.
// In `brandedFaqItems`, on "Are your cleaners screened?" (answer):
// ⚠️ This is the page's gate, not a to-do. See the file header.
// In `brandedFaqItems`, on "Can you work around care routines and meal times?" (answer):
// Brief §25. BFD §9.2 bans em dashes outright. Recast with commas, no
// item lost. Protected otherwise: listing meals, medication rounds,
// physio, visiting hours and rest periods as the things the schedule is
// built backwards from is the operational detail that tells a facility
// manager TLB has done this before.

// §11 Non-branded FAQs, the long-tail block.
//
// ⚠️ VERIFICATION PASS REQUIRED, and this block is the most legally
// sensitive on the whole cluster. Every answer below is deliberately general
// and points the reader at a primary source rather than asserting a standard:
//   - The Aged Care Quality Standards do require a safe, clean and
//     comfortable service environment. Correct in substance, and named
//     without quoting a standard number, since the framework has been
//     revised.
//   - Cleaning is not clinical care and cleaners are not care workers.
//     Correct and important — it is the distinction the last answer makes.
//   - Nothing here should be read as compliance advice, and the answers say
//     so where it matters.
//
// In `nonBrandedFaqItems`, on "How much does aged care cleaning cost?" (answer):
// ⚠️ BRACKETED ON PURPOSE, same as every sibling page.

// §12 Closing CTA.

// CONTENT lives in src/content/pages/commercial-cleaning/aged-care-cleaning.yaml
// and is edited in Pages CMS ("Aged care cleaning"). This file owns the layout. The
// notes above explain why each section says what it says; they stay here
// because Pages CMS rewrites the YAML on save and would drop them.
const page = await getPage(
  'commercial-cleaning/aged-care-cleaning',
  z.object({
    seo: section.seo,
    // The label of the page's main button. It links to the quote page in Site
    // settings. Empty falls back to the site-wide walkthrough label.
    ctaLabel: section.optional(section.text),
    hero: section.hero,
    // Its background photo sits behind the trust bar and this block together
    // (the Backdrop below), so it cannot be left empty.
    definition: section.textBlock.extend({ backgroundImage: section.backgroundPhoto }),
    wrongPage: section.callout,
    scope: section.iconGrid,
    residents: section.storyMosaic,
    worthKnowing: section.textBlock,
    // The pills are the other premises pages, from Site settings.
    related: section.tagCloud.omit({ tags: true }).extend({ note: section.optional(z.array(section.text)) }),
    // The columns are the shared ones in Site settings; the rows are this page's.
    comparison: section.comparisonTable.omit({ columns: true }),
    reviews: section.sectionHeading,
    // The subheading falls back to the shared footprint line, and the towns
    // come from Site settings.
    whereWeClean: section.tagCloud.omit({ tags: true }).extend({ subheading: section.optional(section.text) }),
    // Heading only. The paragraphs are the shared brand lines.
    brandLine: section.sectionHeading,
    brandedFaq: section.faq,
    faq: section.faq,
    closingCta: section.callToAction,
  }),
);

const pageMeta = {
  ...page.seo,
  canonical: 'https://tlbcleaning.com.au/commercial-cleaning/aged-care-cleaning/',
};

const walkthroughCta = { label: page.ctaLabel ?? walkthroughCtaLabel, href: quoteCta.href };

const heroImage = page.hero.image && sized(page.hero.image, '16/9');

const residentsBlocks = page.residents.blocks.map((block) =>
  block.type === 'image' ? { type: 'image' as const, ...sized(block.image, '4/3') } : block,
);

const business = commercialBusiness(pageMeta.description);
---

<Base title={pageMeta.title} description={pageMeta.description} canonical={pageMeta.canonical} business={business}>
  <SiteHeader
    logo={{ label: 'TLB Cleaning', src: logoImg }}
    primaryNav={primaryNav}
    serviceNav={headerNav}
    secondaryCta={headerSecondaryCta}
    cta={quoteCta}
  />

  <main id="main">
    <Hero
      variant="split-single-image"
      imagePosition="left"
      kicker={page.hero.kicker}
      headingLines={page.hero.headingLines}
      subheading={page.hero.subheading}
      lead={page.hero.lead}
      cta={page.hero.cta ?? walkthroughCta}
      image={heroImage}
    />

    <Backdrop image={page.definition.backgroundImage} tint="muted" opacity={90}>
      <TrustBar variant="card" cards={trustBarCards} />
      <TextBlock
        theme="muted"
        align="left"
        heading={page.definition.heading}
        body={page.definition.body}
        cta={page.definition.cta}
      />
    </Backdrop>

    {/* §3 — deliberately above every piece of commercial copy on the page.
        See the file header: the mega-menu label "and seniors" sends family
        members here and the kindest thing this page can do is send them on. */}
    <Callout
      tone="note"
      heading={page.wrongPage.heading}
      body={page.wrongPage.body}
      cta={page.wrongPage.cta}
    />

    <ServiceBlocks
      variant="icon-grid"
      heading={page.scope.heading}
      lead={page.scope.lead}
      note={page.scope.note}
      items={page.scope.items}
      cta={walkthroughCta}
    />

    <StoryMosaic theme="dark" heading={page.residents.heading} blocks={residentsBlocks} cta={page.residents.cta} />

    <TextBlock
      theme="light"
      align="left"
      heading={page.worthKnowing.heading}
      body={page.worthKnowing.body}
      cta={page.worthKnowing.cta}
      backgroundImage={page.worthKnowing.backgroundImage}
    />

    <TagCloud
      heading={page.related.heading}
      subheading={page.related.subheading}
      tags={relatedTags}
      note={page.related.note ?? []}
    />

    <ComparisonTable
      heading={page.comparison.heading}
      cornerLabel={page.comparison.cornerLabel}
      lead={page.comparison.lead}
      columns={comparisonColumns}
      rows={page.comparison.rows}
      footnote={page.comparison.footnote}
      cta={page.comparison.cta}
    />

    <GoogleReviewSlider heading={page.reviews.heading} reviews={googleReviews} profileUrl={googleBusinessProfileUrl} />

    <TagCloud
      heading={page.whereWeClean.heading}
      subheading={page.whereWeClean.subheading ?? footprintSubheading}
      groups={footprintLocationGroups}
      note={page.whereWeClean.note}
    />

    {/* §1.3 The Line as a section heading, with §1.4 as its standfirst.
        The copy, and the reason this page gets `fast` rather than the other
        variant, both live in data/brand-lines.ts. Change it there, not here.

        `muted`, not `dark`: TagCloud above paints --color-brand and Faq below
        paints --color-surface, so Cream between them keeps the page
        alternating instead of stacking two dark bands.

        No CTA prop. §9.5 wants one primary CTA per page, repeated, and the
        closing CallToAction is directly under the FAQs. */}
    <TextBlock
      theme="muted"
      align="left"
      heading={page.brandLine.heading}
      body={[managedForYou.recurring, ...theLineBody.commercial, ...howItWorks.general]}
    />

    {/* Brief §22 and §23. The page carries two FAQ headings and the generic
        one made the split look like a build error. The second heading also
        lands the mapped phrase contiguously, which is the 10-point H2
        component the page previously half scored, and drops Title Case,
        which BFD §9.2 bans. */}
    <Faq heading={page.brandedFaq.heading} lead={page.brandedFaq.lead} items={page.brandedFaq.items} />
    <Faq heading={page.faq.heading} lead={page.faq.lead} items={page.faq.items} />

    <CallToAction
      variant="secondary"
      kicker={page.closingCta.kicker}
      heading={page.closingCta.heading}
      lead={page.closingCta.lead}
      cta={page.closingCta.cta ?? walkthroughCta}
    />
  </main>

  <SiteFooter
    logo={{ label: 'TLB Cleaning', src: footerLogoImg }}
    columns={[
      { title: 'Services', links: footerServiceLinks },
      { title: 'Locations', links: [{ label: 'View all locations', href: '/locations/' }] },
    ]}
    contact={footerContact}
    copyright={footerCopyright}
  />
</Base>
